  m_Name: 6.Marsamxett
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  nameOfPoint: Marsamxett
  coordinatesPoint: {x: 14.510068, y: 35.900497}
  firstPartInfo: "In many ways, Malta\u2019s harbours have been the nexus of its
    history. Across the centuries, these harbours and their strategic location in
    the middle of the Mediterranean Sea have attracted colonisers and conquerors.  
    The plague of 1813 was no exception. On the 28th March of that year, three ships
    arrived from the port of Alexandria, where the plague was raging. One of the
    ships was the Maltese brig San Nicola. A week into its voyage, some of its crew
    members fell ill, so, upon its arrival in Malta, the ship and its crew were quarantined
    at the Lazzaretto over there, across the water, on Manoel Island.  So how did
    the plague escape the quarantine, and arrive at a shoemaker\u2019s home here
    in Valletta - and, from there, spread out to claim the lives of more than four
    thousand people? "
  secondPartInfo: "While the San Nicola was in quarantine, the guards assigned to
    protect it stole some of the linen in its cargo. This linen made its way to a
    wine shop in Sliema - that\u2019s over there, on the other side of Marsamxett
    Harbour. From there, it was picked up by Salvatore Borg, who seems to have had
    a nice sideline to his shoemaking business as a smuggler and fence for stolen
    goods.  Strange to think that some stolen linen led to so many deaths, to months
    of unimaginable tragedy, and, eventually, to Antonio Borg\u2019s execution.   
    Repository of extra documents from the archives Cover of pamphlet from Bonello
    Lazzaretto death notice Bill of lading ship    Under the section Other Administrative
    Documents, maybe see also: document \u2018To Niccolo who brought in plague 1813\u2019,
    from the Officio di Sanita Court case (NAM 92/04/1813, box 3) "
